{:edb/db-before-t 1505 :edb/db-after-t 1506 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534818 50 #inst "2026-10-12T16:20:42.446Z" 13194139534818 17592186045417 true] [17592186124249 1074 "First, we find the partial derivatives: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}((y-4)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)) \\\\  & =\\frac{y-4}{x-2} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}((y-4)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = \\dfrac {y-4}{x-2} \\, \\mathbf i + \\ln(x-2) \\, \\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nNow, we solve $\\nabla f(x,y) = \\mathbf 0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {y-4}{x-2} \\, \\mathbf i + \\ln(x-2) \\, \\mathbf j = 0 \\  \\mathbf i + 0\\ \\mathbf j\n$$\n\nBy equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{i}$ on both sides of the equation (and similarly with $\\mathbf{j}$), we obtain\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-4}{x-2}=0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $y-4=0,$ which means $y=4.$  From the second equation, we obtain $\\ln(x-2)=0,$ which means that $x=3.$\n\nTherefore, $\\nabla f(x,y) =\\mathbf  0$ at the point $(3,4).$" 13194139534818 17592186045417 true] [17592186124249 1074 "First, we find the partial derivatives: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}((y-4)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)) \\\\  & =\\frac{y-4}{x-2} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}((y-4)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = \\dfrac {y-4}{x-2} \\, \\mathbf i + \\ln(x-2) \\, \\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nNow, we solve $\\nabla f(x,y) = \\mathbf 0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {y-4}{x-2} \\, \\mathbf i + \\ln(x-2) \\, \\mathbf j = 0 \\  \\mathbf i + 0\\ \\mathbf j\n$$\n\nBy equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{i}$ on both sides of the equation (and similarly with $\\mathbf{j}$), we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-4}{x-2}=0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $y-4=0,$ which means $y=4.$  From the second equation, we obtain $\\ln(x-2)=0,$ which means that $x=3.$\n\nTherefore, $\\nabla f(x,y) =\\mathbf  0$ at the point $(3,4).$" 13194139534818 17592186045417 false] [17592186124443 1038 #edb/ref 17592186437017 13194139534818 17592186045417 true] [17592186124443 1038 #edb/ref 17592186437019 13194139534818 17592186045417 true] [17592186124444 1038 #edb/ref 17592186437016 13194139534818 17592186045417 true] [17592186124445 1038 #edb/ref 17592186437020 13194139534818 17592186045417 true] [17592186124445 1038 #edb/ref 17592186437021 13194139534818 17592186045417 true] [17592186124447 1038 #edb/ref 17592186437018 13194139534818 17592186045417 true] [17592186124727 1050 1140.0 13194139534818 17592186045417 true] [17592186125727 1064 "For a function of three variables $f(x,y,z)$, the gradient of $f$ is given by the vector\n\n$$\\eqalign{ \\nabla \\! f (x,y,z) &= f_x(x,y,z)\\ \\mathbf i + f_y (x,y,z)\\ \\mathbf j + f_z (x,y,z)\\ \\mathbf k. }$$\n\nFor a function $f(x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n)$ of $n$ variables, its gradient is usually given as a column vector:\n\n$$\\nabla f(x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n) = [\\begin{aligned}{f}_{{x}_{1}} \\\\ {f}_{{x}_{2}} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {f}_{{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]$$" 13194139534818 17592186045417 false] [17592186125727 1064 "For a function of three variables $f(x,y,z)$, the gradient of $f$ is given by the vector  \n\n \n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\nabla \\! f (x,y,z) &= f_x(x,y,z)\\ \\mathbf i + f_y (x,y,z)\\ \\mathbf j + f_z (x,y,z)\\ \\mathbf k.\n                             \n}\n$$\n\n \n\nFor a function $f(x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n)$ of $n$ variables, its gradient is usually given as a column vector:\n\n![](images/21/21e05d85506da576e293bce86b1ca98f4f5b101c49b0591a49d80d2a4b8bfcc1.svg)" 13194139534818 17592186045417 true] [17592186436980 1080 "⟨-1,\\,1⟩" 13194139534818 17592186045417 true] [17592186436980 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534818 17592186045417 true] [17592186436980 1083 #uuid "04308c81-1719-5c33-8d40-