7592186045417 true] [17592186437015 1079 #uuid "fe5a3950-f690-5d04-879c-231b6f838613" 13194139534818 17592186045417 true] [17592186437016 1068 #edb/ref 17592186437015 13194139534818 17592186045417 true] [17592186437016 1069 "q-127946" 13194139534818 17592186045417 true] [17592186437016 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534818 17592186045417 true] [17592186437016 1071 "Evaluate $∇h(x,y,z)$ at $(1,-2,2)$ if $h(x,y,z)={x}^{2}{z}^{2}-x{y}^{2}+2yz.$" 13194139534818 17592186045417 true] [17592186437016 1072 #uuid "3edc7251-4f74-5afa-bcee-22e839f081a7" 13194139534818 17592186045417 true] [17592186437016 1074 "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}{z}^{2}-x{y}^{2}+2yz) \\\\  & =2x{z}^{2}-{y}^{2} \\\\ {h}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}{z}^{2}-x{y}^{2}+2yz) \\\\  & =2z-2xy \\\\ {h}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}{z}^{2}-x{y}^{2}+2yz) \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}z+2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇h(x,y,z) & =⟨{h}_{x}(x,y,z),\\,{h}_{y}(x,y,z),\\,{h}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2x{z}^{2}-{y}^{2},\\,2z-2xy,\\,2{x}^{2}z+2y⟩ \\\\ ∇h(1,-2,2) & =⟨2(1)(2)^{2}-(-2)^{2},\\,-2(1)(-2)+2(2),\\,2(1)^{2}(2)+2(-2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8-4,4+4,4-4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,8,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534818 17592186045417 true] [17592186437017 1068 #edb/ref 17592186437010 13194139534818 17592186045417 true] [17592186437017 1069 "q-127652" 13194139534818 17592186045417 true] [17592186437017 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534818 17592186045417 true] [17592186437017 1071 "Evaluate $∇f(x,y)$ at $(e,1)$ if $f(x,y)={x}^{y}.$" 13194139534818 17592186045417 true] [17592186437017 1072 #uuid "51a75799-7672-5c71-a422-88f2c2c1de7a" 13194139534818 17592186045417 true] [17592186437017 1074 "First, we find the partial derivatives: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{y}) \\\\  & =y{x}^{y-1} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{y}) \\\\  & ={x}^{y}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =y{x}^{y-1}\\text{ }i+{x}^{y}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\text{ }j, \\\\ ∇f(e,1) & =1(e)^{1-1}\\,i+{e}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡e\\,j \\\\  & =i+e\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534818 17592186045417 true] [17592186437018 1068 #edb/ref 17592186437012 13194139534818 17592186045417 true] [17592186437018 1069 "q-71515" 13194139534818 17592186045417 true] [17592186437018 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534818 17592186045417 true] [17592186437018 1071 "What is the gradient of the product $f(x,y)h(x,y)$ if $f(x,y)=x{e}^{y}$ and $h(x,y)=y{e}^{x}?$" 13194139534818 17592186045417 true] [17592186437018 1072 #uuid "5961ee6e-358e-5baa-837e-dbd941cc54b6" 13194139534818 17592186045417 true] [17592186437018 1074 "The gradient of the product of two functions $f(x,y)$ and $h(x,y)$ is given by:\n\n$$\n∇[fh]=f\\,∇\\,h+h\\,∇\\,f\\,\\,\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the partial derivatives of $f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{e}^{y})={e}^{y} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{e}^{y})=x{e}^{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\text{ }j={e}^{y}\\text{ }i+x{e}^{y}\\text{ }j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the partial derivatives of $h(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y{e}^{x})=y{e}^{x} \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y{e}^{x})={e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇h(x,y) & =\\frac{∂h}{∂x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂h}{∂y}\\text{ }j=y{e}^{x}\\text{ }i+{e}^{x}\\text{ }j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the product rule, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇[f(x,y)h(x,y)] & =x{e}^{y}(y{e}^{x}\\text{ }i+{e}^{x}\\text{ }j)+y{e}^{x}({e}^{y}\\text{ }i+x{e}^{y}\\text{ }j) \\\\  & =xy{e}^{x+y}\\text{ }i+x{e}^{x+y}\\text{ }j+y{e}^{x+y}\\text{ }i+xy{e}^{x+y}\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(x+1)y{e}^{x+y}\\text{ }i+x(1+y){e}^{x+y}\\text{ }j\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534818 17592186045417 true] [17592186437019 1068 #edb/ref 17592186437011 13194139534818 17592186045417 true] [17592186437019 1069 "q-71353" 13194139534818 17592186045417 true] [17592186437019 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534818 17592186045417 true] [17592186437019 1071 "Evaluate $∇h(x,y)$ at $(1,-1)$ if $h(x,y)={e}^{{x}^{2}+xy}.$" 13194139534818 17592186045417 true] [17592186437019 1072 #uuid "bace43f6-9821-5763-9bf8-9ca1cf7da037" 13194139534818 17592186045417 true] [17592186437019 1074 "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{{x}^{2}+xy}) \\\\  & =(2x+y){e}^{{x}^{2}+xy} \\\\ {h}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{{x}^{2}+xy}) \\\\  & =x{e}^{{x}^{2}+xy}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the gradient is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇h(x,y) & =⟨{h}_{x}(x,y),\\,{h}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨(2x+y){e}^{{x}^{2}+xy},\\,x{e}^{{x}^{2}+xy}⟩ \\\\ ∇h(1,-1) & =⟨(2(1)+(-1)){e}^{{1}^{2}+(1)(-1)},\\,1\\cdot {e}^{{1}^{2}+(1)(-1)}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534818 17592186045417 true] [17592186437020 1068 #edb/ref 17592186437014 13194139534818 17592186045417 true] [17592186437020 1069 "q-128513" 13194139534818 17592186045417 true] [17592186437020 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534818 17592186045417 true] [17592186437020 1071 "Given $h(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+4yz-2{z}^{2}-2{y}^{2}x.$ Find the points $(x,y,z)$ where $∇h(x,y,z)=0.$" 13194139534818 17592186045417 true] [17592186437020 1072 #uuid "c3503a39-2405-5c8f-9ad7-2d39b5c187d5" 13194139534818 17592186045417 true] [17592186437020 1074 "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+4yz-2{z}^{2}-2{y}^{2}x) \\\\  & =2x-2{y}^{2} \\\\ {h}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+4yz-2{z}^{2}-2{y}^{2}x) \\\\  & =4z-4yx \\\\ {h}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}+4yz-2{z}^{2}-2{y}^{2}x) \\\\  & =4y-4z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇h(x,y,z)=2(x-{y}^{2})\\,i+4(z-yx)\\,j+4(y-z)\\,k.$\n\nNow, we solve $∇f(x,y,z)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(x-{y}^{2})\\,i+4(z-yx)\\,j+4(y-z)\\,k & =0\\text{ }i+0\\text{ }j+0\\text{ }j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by equating the coefficients of $i$, $j$, and $k$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x-{y}^{2}=0, \\\\ z-yx=0, \\\\ y-z=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first equation gives $x={y}^{2}.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we obtain $z={y}^{3}.$ Substituting $z={y}^{3}$ into the third equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}^{3} & =0 \\\\ y(1-{y}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ y & =0,\\,\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For $y=-1,$ we get $x=(-1)^{2}=1$ and $z=(-1)^{3}=-1.$\n\n- For $y=0,$ we obtain $x={0}^{2}=0$ and $z={0}^{3}=0.$\n\n- For $y=1,$ gives $x={1}^{2}=1$ and $z={1}^{3}=1.$\n\nFinally, $∇h(x,y,z)=0$ at three points: $(1,-1,-1),$ $(0,0,0)$ and $(1,1,1).$" 13194139534818 17592186045417 true] [17592186437021 1068 #edb/ref 17592186437013 13194139534818 17592186045417 true] [17592186437021 1069 "q-71470" 13194139534818 17592186045417 true] [17592186437021 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534818 17592186045417 true] [17592186437021 1071 "Given $h(x,y,z)=xy{e}^{x}+{z}^{2}{e}^{z}.$ Find the points $(x,y,z)$ where $∇h(x,y,z)=0.$" 13194139534818 17592186045417 true] [17592186437021 1072 #uuid "d13fce5d-8bd4-547a-afec-720ff5befaa7" 13194139534818 17592186045417 true] [17592186437021 1074 "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy{e}^{x}+{z}^{2}{e}^{z}) \\\\  & =xy{e}^{x}+y{e}^{x}+0 \\\\  & =y(x+1){e}^{x} \\\\ {h}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy{e}^{x}+{z}^{2}{e}^{z}) \\\\  & =x{e}^{x}+0 \\\\  & =x{e}^{x} \\\\ {h}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xy{e}^{x}+{z}^{2}{e}^{z}) \\\\  & =0+2z{e}^{z}+{z}^{2}{e}^{z} \\\\  & =z(2+z){e}^{z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇h(x,y,z)=y(x+1){e}^{x}\\,i+x{e}^{x}\\,j+z(2+z){e}^{z}\\,k.$\n\nNow, we solve $∇f(x,y,z)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x+1){e}^{x}\\,i+x{e}^{x}\\,j+z(2+z){e}^{z}\\,k & =0\\text{ }i+0\\text{ }j+0\\text{ }j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by equating the coefficients of $i$, $j$, and $k$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y(x+1){e}^{x}=0 \\\\ x{e}^{x}=0 \\\\ z(2+z){e}^{z}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe second equation gives $x=0.$ Substituting these into the first equation, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0+1){e}^{0} & =0 \\\\ y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, from the third equation, we get  $z=0$ or $z=-2.$\n\nTherefore, $∇h(x,y,z)=0$ at two points: $(0,0,0)$ and $(0,0,-2).$" 13194139534818 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-04308c81-1719-5c33-8d40-ebccd5767820" 17592186436980 "answer-15a96f20-3d44-594c-b78c-5063ef876b55" 17592186436981 "answer-2e36d0b8-dad3-53f0-905d-1b73b0cfb007" 17592186436982 "answer-3eb3ce4f-3615-5638-9cca-7d5f9a7f4065" 17592186436983 "answer-4a50ec3f-8ef4-53d6-b614-888ffb598364" 17592186436984 "answer-586b4ce1-ff41-581f-8671-763767e3f3aa" 17592186436985 "answer-5b7ebbbc-7508-52dc-91d2-56b705b7eccc" 17592186436986 "answer-6e2a514c-da70-5cc8-95d2-327d72d03570" 17592186436987 "answer-7067d8af-1731-5b47-8f38-e961d32014a4" 17592186436988 "answer-763a0475-ce2e-53a8-aa2d-9466219586f6" 17592186436989 "answer-8e9a1c99-2d0e-5085-aa56-1bc7935a41a6" 17592186436990 "answer-8f042e63-ea21-547d-8187-c6464d907f1c" 17592186436991 "answer-a6cf6f0f-1cc3-5518-a4d7-359da9629347" 17592186436992 "answer-b421e42f-f55a-5ffe-a533-367af3998505" 17592186436993 "answer-b75c28ff-2fd2-5cb7-a89b-d7658bb7354e" 17592186436994 "answer-bd9e8470-5c37-54ae-b712-f0a64e63b2e2" 17592186436995 "answer-c0ae3e99-1ea5-5fa4-a1e9-7139e200dfcc" 17592186436996 "answer-ca48ce3d-8118-5a85-8d40-9f54dccebe39" 17592186436997 "answer-caf56146-05fe-53cc-92f2-a535ba4943df" 17592186436998 "answer-d2acb1a7-39df-54ef-82c2-34bde846d73e" 17592186436999 "answer-d8f83b16-7c14-555d-b3bd-510c637f070d" 17592186437000 "answer-df8dc6a7-9339-5e43-b61c-4513120ec263" 17592186437001 "answer-e58c9d0d-900d-5547-a140-989e42e2459e" 17592186437002 "answer-e717d850-f7b3-516d-8057-a5abe63141f9" 17592186437003 "answer-e7d9044c-103d-5c75-a1cc-b44786993ed3" 1759218643700